  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through Wednesday)\nIssued at 230 PM MDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nThe upper low will move into eastern ID and western WY on Saturday\nwith a speed max aloft (50-60 kt at 300 mb) still overlaying \neastern NM. This will introduce more dry air from the west which \nwill lower the overall heavy rainfall threat in western and most\nof central NM, however an upper level trough axis will extend from\nthe ID/WY low into the Four Corners, providing additional large\nscale ascent. This will still allow storms to develop over the\nDivide, but they will be higher-based and not as efficient at\nrainfall production. Deeper moisture and the speed max aloft of the\nsouth central mountains and east central plains will help produce\nmore rain-efficient storms there Saturday afternoon.\n\nThe low will move farther east into the interface of the northern\nRockies and northern Great Plains on Sunday, filling in and\nweakening. As a result, lighter and drier westerly winds aloft \nwill feed into NM, shutting down storm potential for most zones. \nThe exception will be a backdoor front that will enter \nnortheastern to east central NM, providing some low layer moisture\nadvection and surface convergence for a few isolated storms. \n\nThe front looks to make a stronger push Sunday night into Monday,\novertaking most of the eastern half of NM. Isolated to scattered\nstorms would be favored behind this boundary where moisture would\nbe more readily available Monday afternoon, and the coverage would\nincrease through the evening. Through this time, our attention\nwill also be drawn upstream toward a new Pacific low/trough that \nwill be crossing and lifting northeast of the lower CO river basin\nwhile high pressure aloft settles near the TX coast. This will\nonce again squeeze and draw moisture northward into NM, merging\nwith what the backdoor boundary had already brought in from the \nnortheast. While there are model discrepancies on how exactly the\ntrough will evolve and move, there should be a southerly flow \naloft over NM, leading to a more active storm period Tuesday into \nWednesday of next week. Along with the significant uptick in \nrainfall, a few strong to severe storms will be possible, \nespecially on Tuesday when directional shear appears more \nfavorable.",
